%I A137311 #23 Oct 06 2026 09:07:23
%S A137311 2,12,20,26,36,42,84,92,98,108,114,132,140,164,188,194,212,218,234,
%T A137311 236,258,260,276,290,306,314,324,348,362,372,380,386,402,426,428,444,
%U A137311 474,476,482,506,524,548,570,572,602,644,674,692,698,714,716,738,740,764
%N A137311 Numbers k such that a type-5 Gaussian normal basis over GF(2^k) exists.
%C A137311 A type-t Gaussian normal basis exists for GF(2^k) if and only if p = k*t+1 is prime and gcd(k, (p-1)/ord(2 mod p)) = 1.
%C A137311 From _Jianing Song_, Oct 06 2026: (Start)
%C A137311 Note that p = k*t+1 being prime and gcd(k, (p-1)/ord(2 mod p)) = 1 imply that (p-1)/ord(2 mod p) divides t. In particular, k is a term in this sequence if and only if k satisfies one the following conditions:
%C A137311 (i) ord(2 mod p) = p-1 (k = 2, 12, 20, 26, 36, ...);
%C A137311 (ii) k is not divisible by 5, and ord(2 mod p) = (p-1)/5 (k = 114, 194, 236, 362, 402, ...). (End)
%H A137311 Jianing Song, <a href="/A137311/b137311.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A137311 Joerg Arndt, <a href="https://www.jjj.de/fxt/#fxtbook">Matters Computational (The Fxtbook)</a>, section 42.9 "Gaussian normal bases", pp.914-920.
%o A137311 (PARI) istypet(n,t=5) = my(p); (isprime(p=n*t+1) && gcd(n, (p-1)/znorder(Mod(2, p))) == 1) \\ _Jianing Song_, Oct 06 2026, following _Michel Marcus_'s program in A101284
%Y A137311 Cf. A071642 (type-1), A054639 (type-2), A136415 (type-3), A137310 (type-4), A137313 (type-6), A137314 (type-7), A101284 (type-8).
%K A137311 nonn,changed
%O A137311 1,1
